course: books
lesson: 17
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter IX, part 1: The Mock Turtle's Story (the Duchess and her morals)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Out of prison, the Duchess is suddenly sweet to Alice: «You dear old thing!» She tucks her arm into Alice's and walks with her. Alice wonders if it was only the pepper that made her so savage in the kitchen, and invents a rule of her own: pepper makes people hot-tempered, vinegar makes them sour, and barley-sugar makes children sweet-tempered.
explain: The Duchess has a new habit: everything has «a moral», a lesson, and she finds one in every remark, even when it makes no sense. She keeps leaning her very sharp chin on Alice's shoulder, which Alice bears politely, though she doesn't like it.
explain: The morals get sillier: mustard and flamingoes both «bite», mustard is a mineral from a «mustard-mine», and then a moral so long and tangled that nobody could follow it. «I make you a present of everything I've said,» says the Duchess, and Alice thinks: a cheap sort of present!
explain: Suddenly the Queen stands in front of them, frowning like a thunderstorm: «either you or your head must be off». The Duchess takes her choice and disappears at once. The game goes on until the soldiers have arrested every player except the King, the Queen and Alice, and there are no arches left.
explain: Out of breath, the Queen asks Alice if she has seen the Mock Turtle, «the thing Mock Turtle Soup is made from», and takes her to meet him. As they leave, Alice hears the King quietly tell everyone «You are all pardoned», and she feels glad.
fact: The Duchess greets Alice: «You can't think how glad I am to see you again, you dear old thing!»
fact: Alice thinks «perhaps it was only the pepper that had made her so savage» (savage = fierce and cruel) in the kitchen in chapter VI.
fact: Alice's new rule: pepper makes people hot-tempered, vinegar sour, camomile bitter, and barley-sugar (a hard sweet) makes children sweet-tempered.
fact: «Everything's got a moral, if only you can find it,» says the Duchess; a moral is the lesson a story teaches.
fact: The Duchess is «exactly the right height to rest her chin upon Alice's shoulder, and it was an uncomfortably sharp chin». The book also calls her «very ugly»: judging people by their looks is an old habit we do not admire today.
fact: «Oh, 'tis love, 'tis love, that makes the world go round!» ('tis = it is); Alice whispers that somebody said it was done by everybody minding their own business — the Duchess herself, in the kitchen.
fact: «Take care of the sense, and the sounds will take care of themselves» is a joke on the real saying «Take care of the pence, and the pounds will take care of themselves».
fact: The Duchess says she won't put her arm round Alice's waist because she is «doubtful about the temper of your flamingo».
fact: «Flamingoes and mustard both bite» — mustard «bites» your tongue with its hot taste; the moral «Birds of a feather flock together» doesn't fit, as Alice notes: «Only mustard isn't a bird».
fact: The Duchess agrees with everything Alice says: mustard is «a mineral», then she agrees it is «a vegetable»; really mustard is made from the seeds of a plant.
fact: «The more there is of mine, the less there is of yours» plays on «mine», a hole you dig minerals from, and «mine», belonging to me.
fact: Her longest moral, «Never imagine yourself not to be otherwise than what it might appear to others...», goes on so long, full of «not» and «otherwise», that it cancels itself out and nobody can follow it.
fact: «I make you a present of everything I've said as yet»; Alice thinks «A cheap sort of present!» but does not say it aloud.
fact: «I've a right to think,» says Alice; «Just about as much right as pigs have to fly,» says the Duchess — then the Queen appears and her voice dies away.
fact: The Queen: «either you or your head must be off, and that in about half no time! Take your choice!» The Duchess «took her choice, and was gone in a moment».
fact: While the Queen was away, the other guests had been resting in the shade; they hurry back to the game the moment they see her.
fact: After half an hour all the players except the King, the Queen and Alice are «in custody» (arrested); the soldiers who held them can no longer be arches.
fact: Mock turtle soup was a real Victorian dish: a cheaper copy of turtle soup, usually made from a calf's head; «mock» means pretend. Carroll invents a creature it is «made from».
fact: As they walk off, the King says quietly to the company «You are all pardoned», and Alice is glad, because she had felt quite unhappy at the number of executions the Queen had ordered.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «cwtch» (Wales) = a cuddle; «gadgie» (North East England) = a man, a bloke; «rizz» = charm, being good at making people like you; «adulting» = doing grown-up jobs.
exercise: How does the Duchess greet Alice this time? | «You can't think how glad I am to see you again, you dear old thing!» | «Off with her head!» | «Pig!» | «You don't know much, and that's a fact»
exercise: What does Alice think made the Duchess so savage in the kitchen? | Perhaps only the pepper | The baby's crying, which went on all morning without a single break | The cook's frying-pan | The Queen's letter
exercise: In Alice's new rule, what makes people hot-tempered? | Pepper | Vinegar | Camomile | Barley-sugar
exercise: In Alice's rule, what makes children sweet-tempered? | Barley-sugar and such things | Pepper and mustard mixed together in a big pot of soup | Vinegar | Treacle from a well
exercise: What is the Duchess fond of finding in everything? | A moral | A mistake | A pepper-pot | A riddle with no answer
exercise: Why does Alice not like keeping so close to the Duchess? | Her sharp chin digs into Alice's shoulder | She smells strongly of pepper and onions from the kitchen fire | She is too tall | She keeps singing
exercise: «'Tis love, 'tis love, that makes the world go round!» What does «'tis» mean? | It is | It was | This is | Tis is a name
exercise: Alice whispers that somebody once said the world goes round by «everybody minding their own business». Who said it? | The Duchess herself, in the kitchen | The Cheshire Cat, on the branch of a tree | The Caterpillar | The Queen
exercise: What reason does the Duchess give for not putting her arm round Alice's waist? | She is doubtful about the temper of the flamingo | She is afraid of the Queen seeing them walking arm in arm | Her arm is tired | She doesn't like Alice
exercise: «Flamingoes and mustard both bite.» How does mustard «bite»? | Its hot taste stings your tongue | It has tiny teeth that you can see if you look very closely | It jumps out of the pot | It is a kind of bird
exercise: The Duchess gives the moral «Birds of a feather flock together». What does Alice point out? | «Only mustard isn't a bird» | «Flamingoes never flock together at all» | «That isn't a proper moral at all» | «But birds can't bite anybody»
exercise: What does the Duchess say is near here? | A large mustard-mine | A deep old treacle-well | The Queen's dark prison | The March Hare's house
exercise: How does the Duchess answer whenever Alice says what mustard is? | She agrees with everything Alice says | She argues and says Alice is wrong every time | She asks the Queen | She falls asleep
exercise: «I make you a present of everything I've said as yet.» What does Alice think? | «A cheap sort of present!» | «What a kind lady» | «I must write it down» | «That's worth a lot of money»
exercise: Does Alice say «A cheap sort of present!» out loud? | No, she only thinks it | Yes, very loudly, and the Duchess laughs and shakes her hand | Yes, in a whisper | She writes it down
exercise: «I've a right to think.» What does the Duchess answer? | «Just about as much right as pigs have to fly» | «Everyone has a right to think, my dear, even a Duchess» | «Then think of a moral» | «Off with your head»
exercise: Why does the Duchess suddenly stop in the middle of the word «moral»? | The Queen is standing in front of them, frowning like a thunderstorm | The flamingo has bitten her finger hard and she is crying out in pain | Alice has walked away | She has forgotten the moral
exercise: What choice does the Queen give the Duchess? | Either she or her head must be off | Play croquet or go back to prison for ever | Make soup or make tarts | Leave the garden or say sorry
exercise: What does the Duchess choose? | She is gone in a moment | She stays and argues back | She asks the King for help | She lets the Queen take her head
exercise: What had the other guests done while the Queen was away? | Rested in the shade | Run home | Painted the rest of the white roses red | Eaten the tarts
exercise: Why are there no arches left after half an hour? | The soldiers had to stop being arches to arrest the players | The flamingoes knocked them all down while flying up into the trees | The Queen sent them home | It got dark
exercise: Who is NOT under sentence of execution by the end of the game? | The King, the Queen and Alice | The White Rabbit, the Duchess and the Knave | The gardeners | The hedgehogs
exercise: «It's the thing Mock Turtle Soup is made from.» What was real mock turtle soup? | A cheaper copy of turtle soup, usually made from a calf's head | Soup made from real turtles found in the sea near the Queen's garden | Soup with pepper only | Cold soup
exercise: What does the King say quietly as Alice walks off? | «You are all pardoned» | «Off with all their heads» | «Consider your verdict» | «Fetch the executioner»
exercise: How does Alice feel when she hears the King? | Glad, because she was unhappy about all the executions | Cross, because she wanted the Queen to win and the players to lose their heads | Bored | Sleepy
test: Finn: «the Duchess was dead mean again, slapping Alice.» Put it right. | She was very friendly now, calling her «you dear old thing» | Right: she boxed Alice's ears for being late to croquet and called her a pig | Right: she threw a plate at her | She ignored Alice completely
test: Why is Alice's «rule» about pepper and barley-sugar a funny one? | She invents a «rule» from one guess, and sour, bitter and sweet are both tastes and moods | It is a real rule from her schoolbooks, tested by scientists | The Duchess told her it | Pepper really does make you sweet
test: What does «Everything's got a moral, if only you can find it» show about the Duchess now? | She is so keen on morals that she finds one even where there is no sense | She is a very wise teacher who explains things clearly, one moral at a time, in good order | She hates stories | She is still angry with Alice
test: Ollie: «the Duchess said take care of the pence and the pounds sort themselves.» Put it right. | She said «Take care of the sense, and the sounds will take care of themselves» — a joke on that saying | Right, she said it word for word, to teach Alice how to save money | She said nothing about money or sense | She said it about mustard
test: What would happen if you tried to follow the Duchess's very long moral, «Never imagine yourself not to be otherwise than...»? | You'd get lost: it is a tangle of «not» and «otherwise» that cancels itself out | You'd learn a clear, short rule about how to behave politely at a croquet game with the Queen | You'd learn about mustard | You'd find the answer to the raven riddle
test: The Duchess agrees mustard is a mineral, then agrees it is a vegetable. What does this show? | She agrees with whatever Alice says, without thinking | She knows a great deal about mustard and minerals from her own mustard-mine | She is trying to teach Alice science | She wants to argue
test: «The more there is of mine, the less there is of yours.» Which two meanings of «mine» does it use? | A hole where minerals are dug, and «belonging to me» | A kind of bomb hidden underground, and a kind of yellow mustard | My mind, and my mouth | A mine of gold, and a mine of coal
test: Leo: «she's got mad rizz, the Duchess, Alice loved her chin on her shoulder.» (rizz = charm) Put it right. | Alice didn't like it, but bore it politely so as not to be rude | Right: Alice asked her to rest her chin there | Alice pushed her away | Alice laughed and hugged her in a big cwtch (cuddle)
test: «Just about as much right as pigs have to fly.» What does the Duchess mean? | Alice has no right to think at all, as pigs can't fly | Alice has every right to think, as pigs can fly perfectly well in Wonderland | Pigs are very clever thinkers | Alice should think about pigs
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «The Duchess argued with the Queen and won.» | False: she «took her choice» and ran off in a moment | True: she told the Queen off firmly and the Queen said sorry to her | True: the King helped her | False: she lost her head
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, what did the Duchess shout at the baby that made Alice jump? | «Pig!» | «Wow! wow! wow!» | «Chop off her head!» | «Mind your own business!»
test: Looking back: what happened to the Duchess's baby in chapter VI? | It turned into a pig and trotted off into the wood | It turned into a pink flamingo and flew to the Queen's garden | It fell asleep in the pepper | It became the Knave
test: Looking back: «If everybody minded their own business, the world would go round a deal faster.» What did Alice try to explain then? | That the earth takes twenty-four hours to turn round on its axis | That the earth is flat and stands still in the middle of the sky | That the Queen rules the world | That pepper is bad for babies
test: Looking back: the Duchess answered «Talking of axes, chop off her head!» in chapter VI. What was the pun? | Alice said «axis», and the Duchess heard «axes», tools for chopping | Alice said «taxes», and the Duchess wanted money | Alice said «ax», which is a bird | There was no pun
test: Looking back: in chapter VIII the Duchess was in prison. Why, according to the White Rabbit? | She boxed the Queen's ears | She stole the tarts | She threw the baby | She played croquet badly
test: Looking back: «mock» means pretend. Who loved to pretend in chapter I, even playing croquet against herself? | Alice, «very fond of pretending to be two people» | The White Rabbit, who pretended to be a footman in a waistcoat | Dinah the cat | The Queen of Hearts
test: ★★★ Alice's rule: pepper → hot-tempered, vinegar → sour, camomile → bitter, barley-sugar → sweet. What do the four words describe? | Both a taste and a person's mood | Only foods you can buy | Four kinds of soup | Four of the Duchess's morals
test: ★★★ «Birds of a feather flock together» is said about flamingoes and mustard. What is wrong with the moral as a piece of logic? | It is about birds, but one of the two things is not a bird | It is right, as mustard and flamingoes are both pink and fly | Flamingoes do not have feathers | Nothing is wrong
test: ★★★ When the game stops, every player is in custody except three. If eight players had begun, how many are under arrest? | 5 | 8 | 3 | 11
exam: Identify the conjecture formed by Alice in respect of the Duchess's former savagery. | That it was occasioned merely by the pepper | That it proceeded from fear of the Queen | That it was owing to the Cheshire Cat | That the Duchess was unwell
exam: It is stated that the Duchess was confronted by the Queen. Select the response which most accurately characterises the alternatives proposed. | The removal either of the Duchess or of her head | Payment of a fine or imprisonment for a year and a day | A game of croquet or a game of cards | An apology or banishment to the sea
exam: Ascertain the substance from which, according to the Queen, Mock Turtle Soup is prepared. | The Mock Turtle | Pepper and treacle | Roasted flamingoes | Hedgehogs and mustard
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the King's conduct as Alice departed? | He pardoned all persons under sentence, speaking in a low voice | He ordered further executions | He accompanied Alice to the Mock Turtle | He beheaded the Duchess
